# ddai5  —  100.117.227.72
# RTX PRO 4500 Blackwell, 32.6 GB, sm_120
#
# BULK / EXTRACTION BOX, identical hardware to ddai1-ddai4. Activate with
# SERVER_PROFILE=72.
#
# NOW INHERITS. This file used to be a deliberate near-copy of server-63.env,
# on the reasoning that keeping the two byte-identical made `diff` show exactly
# what was host-specific. That held while there were two such boxes; at five it
# is the drift the profile system exists to prevent, and the model swap to
# Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-NVFP4 is precisely the edit that would have had to be made in
# five files and missed in one. The base is what ddai1-ddai4 already share.
#
# WHAT INHERITING CHANGED HERE, beyond the model: utilization 0.80 -> 0.84 and
# max-num-seqs 32 -> 64. Both are the base's measured values and both were
# already argued for in this file's own comments — the note that "at 0.86 the
# queue stayed empty and the corpus ran ~2x faster" was sitting above a 0.80.
# Re-measure on first boot here as on every box, per the base's warning.
#
# ONE chat instance, not three. The model plus its KV cache and Infinity fill
# the card; there is no room for a second. Delegate instead of splitting — see
# the commented block at the bottom of the base.
EXTENDS=common-blackwell-32gb

SERVER_NAME=ddai5
# LITELLM_PORT is inherited (8090), as on ddai1-ddai4, so the box differs from
# the base by its name alone and every consumer addresses the fleet the same
# way. This file used to pin 8080. That pin was valid — the platform moved its
# traefik dashboard to 8081 on 2026-09-18 so LiteLLM could keep 8080 on GPU
# hosts (project-deepdarshak spec 2026-09-18-deconflict-declared-port-defaults)
# — but on 2026-09-25 ddai5 was still running a traefik container from BEFORE
# that change, holding 127.0.0.1:8080, so 8080 failed to bind here. 8090 was
# chosen then and kept: consumers (ember's GPU_DDAI5_URL) are wired to it.
